WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.410
Reefs are buildups constructed by corals and other organisms and by

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.770
accumulated carbonate material. The warm-water reef communities emphasized here thrive in

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:14.090
clear, shallow water. Too much suspended sediment can block light, bury

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:16.070
organisms or clog their openings.

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:20.570
Waves break and rework carbonate skeletons, supplying the white sand around

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:24.670
many reef islands. Reef growth is a biological and sedimentary process.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.250
A fringing reef grows along or very close to a shoreline.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.930
A barrier reef lies farther offshore, with a lagoon on its

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:37.870
landward side. Broad carbonate platforms, such as the Bahamas, accumulate shallow-water

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.570
carbonate over a wider area.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.170
The Great Barrier Reef source example also shows upward growth as

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.130
rising sea level flooded a platform.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.870
Do not confuse the lagoon with the open ocean.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.980
In the subsiding-volcano model, a tropical volcanic island first develops a

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.340
fringing reef. After magmatism wanes, cooling allows the supporting plate and

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.780
island to sink. Coral construction continues upward while the volcanic foundation

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.120
moves downward. Both motions matter:

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.460
simply sinking the entire reef would drown it instead of producing

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.060
a living shallow-water rim.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.440
As the island subsides, a lagoon separates the reef from the

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.060
shrinking shoreline. Eventually the volcano falls below sea level, leaving an

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.200
atoll around a central lagoon if reef growth keeps pace.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.640
The process takes geological time;

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.380
this animation compresses it. The textbook also mentions a carbonate-platform erosion

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.460
model for some atolls, so Darwin's sequence is a model, not

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:33.920
every possible carbonate history.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.700
Use the distribution map to connect setting with process.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.160
Examples include the Caribbean, Florida and the Bahamas, the Great Barrier

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.180
Reef, Pacific islands, East Africa, the Maldives and the Red Sea.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.160
Warm, clear, shallow water helps explain the tropical pattern and why

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.120
the cold Aleutian setting is different.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.220
Identify reef geometry, environmental conditions and growth history as separate pieces

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:57.720
of evidence.
